#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:4
msgid "Firewall Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:7
msgid "Router Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:10
msgid "Port Forwards"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:12
msgid ""
"If you have a NAT router which supports UPnP, the easiest way to get a "
"working port forward is to make sure UPnP setting is enabled on both "
"Syncthing and the router – Syncthing will try to handle the rest. If it "
"succeeds you will see a message in the console saying::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:17
msgid "Created UPnP port mapping for external port XXXXX on UPnP device YYYYY."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:19
msgid ""
"If this is not possible or desirable, you should set up a port forwarding "
"for ports **22000/TCP** and **22000/UDP** (or whichever port is set in the "
"*Sync Protocol Listen Address* setting). The external forwarded ports and "
"the internal destination ports have to be the same (e.g. 22000/TCP)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:24
msgid ""
"Communication in Syncthing works both ways. Therefore if you set up port "
"forwards for one device, other devices will be able to connect to it even "
"when they are behind a NAT network or firewall."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:28
msgid ""
"In the absence of port forwarding, :ref:`relaying` may work well enough to "
"get devices connected and synced, but will perform poorly in comparison to a "
"direct connection."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:33
msgid "Local Discovery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:35
msgid ""
"The router needs to allow/forward broad-/multicasts for local discovery to "
"work. Usually these are allowed by default in a single local subnet, but may "
"be blocked between different subnets or even between a bridged Wi-Fi and LAN."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:39
msgid ""
"If you are unable to set up your router thus or your firewall as shown "
"below, and your devices have static IP addresses, you can specify them "
"directly by changing the default ``dynamic`` setting for *Addresses* to "
"something like: ``tcp://192.168.1.xxx:22000, dynamic``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:45
msgid "Local Firewall"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:47
msgid ""
"If your PC has a local firewall, you will need to open the following ports "
"for incoming and outgoing traffic:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:50
msgid "Port **22000/TCP**: TCP based sync protocol traffic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:51
msgid "Port **22000/UDP**: QUIC based sync protocol traffic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:52
msgid ""
"Port **21027/UDP**: for discovery broadcasts on IPv4 and multicasts on IPv6"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:54
msgid ""
"If you configured a custom port in the *Sync Protocol Listen Address* "
"setting, you have to adapt the firewall rules accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:58
msgid "Uncomplicated Firewall (ufw)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:59
msgid ""
"If you're using ``ufw`` on Linux and have installed the `Syncthing package "
"<https://apt.syncthing.net/>`__, you can allow the necessary ports by "
"running::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:62
msgid "sudo ufw allow syncthing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:64
msgid ""
"If you also want to allow external access to the Syncthing web GUI, run::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:66
msgid "sudo ufw allow syncthing-gui"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:68
msgid ""
"Allowing external access is **not**  necessary for a typical installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:70
msgid "You can then verify that the ports mentioned above are allowed::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:72
msgid "sudo ufw status verbose"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:74
msgid ""
"In case you installed Syncthing manually you can follow the `instructions to "
"manually add the syncthing preset <https://github.com/syncthing/syncthing/"
"tree/main/etc/firewall-ufw>`__ to ufw."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:78
msgid "Firewalld"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:79
msgid ""
"If you are using `Firewalld <https://firewalld.org/>`__ it has included "
"support for syncthing (since version 0.5.0, January 2018), and you can "
"enable it with::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:83
msgid ""
"sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-service=syncthing --permanent\n"
"sudo firewall-cmd --reload"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:86
msgid "Similarly there is also a ``syncthing-gui`` service."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:90
msgid "Remote Web GUI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:92
msgid ""
"To be able to access the web GUI from other computers, you need to change "
"the *GUI Listen Address* setting from the default ``127.0.0.1:8384`` to "
"``0.0.0.0:8384``. You also need to open the port in your local firewall if "
"you have one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:98
msgid "Tunneling via SSH"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:100
msgid ""
"If you have SSH access to the machine running Syncthing but would rather not "
"open the web GUI port to the outside world, you can access it through a SSH "
"tunnel instead. You can start a tunnel with a command like the following::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:104
msgid "ssh -L 9999:localhost:8384 machine"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:106
msgid ""
"This will bind to your local port 9999 and forward all connections from "
"there to port 8384 on the target machine. This still works even if Syncthing "
"is bound to listen on localhost only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:111
msgid "Via a Proxy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/firewall.rst:113
msgid ""
"Syncthing can use a SOCKS5 proxy for outbound connections. Please see :ref:"
"`proxying`."
msgstr ""
